# Both image builds send their context whole to the builder: the engine from
# `engine/`, the Studio server from the repository root with
# `-f server/Dockerfile`. Without this file a local build uploads ~940MB, of
# which ~790MB is installed dependencies that every image reinstalls for
# itself anyway.
#
# CI never noticed, because a fresh `actions/checkout` has no `node_modules`
# and no build output to send. Only a working checkout does, which is exactly
# the case a local build-and-publish loop exercises.
#
# Nothing excluded here is source. The server build needs `package.json`,
# `package-lock.json`, `packages/`, `server/` and `desktop/`; all four stay.
# (A context rooted at `engine/` reads `engine/.dockerignore`, not this file --
# Docker resolves the ignore file from the context root. The engine tree
# carries no installed dependencies, so it needs none.)

# Installed dependencies. `npm ci` inside the image installs its own, and this
# host's tree is arm64 -- copying it into a linux/amd64 image would produce
# native modules that cannot exec on the node.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/

# Build output, all of it generated by the image build itself. A stale host
# `server/dist` or `server/web` copied into the context would be silently
# preferred over what the build produces, pinning the image to whatever was
# last built on the workstation.
**/dist/
desktop/release/
server/web/

# Local-only state with no bearing on any image.
.git/
graphify-out/
logs/
coverage/
.act/
*.log
.DS_Store
